course: technology
lesson: 31
title: Roads and road signs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A road looks like a flat black strip, but it is really a layer cake. At the bottom is hard-rolled crushed stone that spreads the weight of lorries, and on top is asphalt, a hot mix of small stones and sticky black bitumen, rolled flat while it is still warm.
explain: Water is a road's worst enemy. On a winter night water seeps into a tiny crack, freezes, swells like a bottle of water left in the freezer and pushes the crack wider; tyres then knock the broken bits out, and a pothole is born.
explain: Roads also talk to drivers without words. Shapes and colours on signs, white lines with glittering glass beads, cat's eyes that shine back in the headlights and bumps outside a school all tell a driver what to do in a split second.
explain: The safety rule for every road is the same: roadworks and motorways are for vehicles and trained crews, never for walking or playing.
fact: A road is built in layers: crushed stone rolled hard at the bottom, then hot asphalt, a mix of stones and sticky black bitumen, spread and rolled flat on top; people often call it tarmac.
fact: Potholes grow after winter because water gets into cracks, freezes, expands and breaks the road, and traffic then knocks pieces out; councils fill them, and cyclists watch for them.
fact: Road lines are painted with special paint that has tiny glass beads in it, so the lines reflect headlights at night.
fact: Cat's eyes, the little reflectors set into the middle of the road, were invented by Percy Shaw in England in 1934.
fact: A motorway has no crossings and no traffic lights, only gentle bends, several lanes and slip roads to join and leave; the first UK motorway, the Preston Bypass, opened in 1958.
fact: Never walk on a motorway; if a car breaks down, everyone gets out on the side away from the traffic and waits behind the barrier.
fact: At a roundabout drivers give way to traffic already coming round, so cars keep moving instead of stopping at lights and only stop when something is coming, not because a timer says so; Swindon's Magic Roundabout is made of five small roundabouts around one big one.
fact: Street lights switch on at dusk because a light sensor notices the light fading; many are now LEDs, and some dim late at night to save energy.
fact: Speed bumps, sometimes called sleeping policemen, are raised parts of the road that make drivers slow down, which is why they are put near schools.
fact: In the UK and much of Europe, circular signs give orders, triangular signs warn and rectangular signs give information, and a reflective coating makes them shine at night.
fact: A gritter lorry spreads salt before a frost, because salty water freezes at a lower temperature than plain water, so ice forms less easily.
fact: Temporary traffic lights at roadworks take turns letting one direction of traffic through a single lane, using timers or sensors.
exercise: What is the black top layer of most roads made of? | Asphalt, a mix of stones and sticky bitumen | Black-painted concrete slabs laid side by side | Melted-down old car tyres pressed flat and smooth | Coal dust mixed with sand and a little water
exercise: Why are there more potholes after winter? | Water in cracks freezes, expands and breaks the road | Cold air makes the asphalt shrink away until it is completely gone | Snowploughs dig holes in the road on purpose | Winter tyres are heavier and sink in
exercise: What makes white road lines shine at night? | Tiny glass beads mixed into the paint | A small battery hidden under each line | Glow-in-the-dark paint charged by day | Water lying on top of the paint
exercise: Who invented cat's eyes for roads, and when? | Percy Shaw, in 1934 | Henry Ford, the car maker, in 1913 | Isaac Newton, in 1687 | Tim Berners-Lee, in 1989
exercise: Why are there no traffic lights on a motorway? | It has no crossings, so cars never need to stop | Lights would be too high to see at speed, so drivers would miss them | Motorways are only used at night | Drivers on motorways are always experts
exercise: Your family car breaks down on a motorway. What should everyone do? | Get out on the side away from traffic and wait behind the barrier | Stay in the car with the seat belts on and wait | Walk along the hard shoulder to the next town | Stand behind the car and wave at drivers
exercise: When was the first UK motorway, the Preston Bypass, opened? | 1958 | 1858 | 1928 | 1998
exercise: How do drivers keep moving at a roundabout? | They give way to traffic already coming round, then flow on | They all stop and wait for a green light | The biggest vehicle always goes first, and everyone else waits for it to clear | They drive round as fast as they can
exercise: How does a street light know it is getting dark? | A light sensor notices the light fading | A worker switches every light on by hand | It is set to come on at 6 o'clock every day | It feels the air getting colder
exercise: Why are speed bumps often put near schools? | To make drivers slow down where children walk | To stop big lorries from using the road at all | To drain the rainwater off the road into the gutter | To show exactly where the school bus has to stop
exercise: On UK road signs, what does a triangle usually mean? | A warning of something ahead | An order that you must always obey | Information about places and how far they are | A sign that is for cyclists only
exercise: Why does a gritter spread salt before a frost? | Salty water freezes at a lower temperature, so ice forms less | Salt makes the road warmer by burning slowly | Salt soaks up all the water like a sponge | Salt makes tyres sticky on ice
exercise: How do temporary lights at roadworks help? | They take turns letting one direction through a single lane | They tell drivers the speed limit | They light up the road for the workers at night, so they can see what they dig | They warn pedestrians to cross quickly
exercise: Why is a road built in layers, with hard stone at the bottom? | The stone spreads the weight of heavy lorries | The stone keeps the road warm in winter so ice can't form | The stone makes the road quieter for the houses nearby | The stone is cheaper than air, so the road costs less
exercise: What is another name for a speed bump? | A sleeping policeman | A cat's eye in the road | A slip road onto a motorway | A hard shoulder by the kerb
test: «my uncle says potholes are just from lorries being too heavy, nothing else» What is the best correction? | Lorries knock pieces out, but freezing water in cracks breaks the road first | He is right, weather never matters | Potholes are dug by animals at night | Potholes come from the Sun melting the road
test: In a cartoon, a cat walks down the middle of the road and its glowing eyes guide cars. What are real cat's eyes? | Small reflectors set into the road that shine back in headlights | Lamps powered by wires under the road | Real animals trained by the council | Painted dots that glow on their own
test: «mate said we can walk on the motorway, it's quicker to the shop» What is the right answer? | Never walk on a motorway; it is only for vehicles | It is fine if you walk on the white line in the middle, where the cars can see you | It is fine in the early morning | It is fine if you wear a coat
test: What would happen if a cold country stopped gritting its roads? | More ice would form on roads in frosts, and more cars would skid | Roads would stay dry because ice cannot form on asphalt | Nothing, salt does not change ice at all | The roads would melt in winter
test: You see a red circle sign with a number 20 in it. What kind of sign is it? | An order: the speed limit is 20 | A warning about 20 children | Information that a town is 20 miles away | A sign for bus route 20
test: Count them: Swindon's Magic Roundabout has 5 small roundabouts. If you drive round 2 of them on the way to school and the same 2 on the way home, how many roundabouts have you gone round that day? | 4 | 2 | 5 | 10
test: Count them: a road has 3 lanes each way. How many lanes is that in total? | 6 | 3 | 9 | 4
test: «street light came on and it's only 4 o'clock, broken innit» What is the likely reason? | In winter it gets dark early, and the light sensor noticed | Street lights come on at 4 every day | Someone at the council pressed the wrong button for the whole town by mistake | It turned on because it got cold
test: «crap, there's a crew digging the road, can we go watch close up» What is the right rule? | Keep out of roadworks and follow the signs | Go close if the machines are switched off | Go close if you wear a bike helmet and stay behind the orange cones | Go close but only for a minute
test: Why do roundabouts often keep traffic moving better than lights at a quiet junction? | Cars only stop when something is coming, not on a timer | Roundabouts make cars drive faster than the limit | Cars can go round as many times as they like until they find a gap in the traffic | Lights stop all traffic for hours
test: «why's the road all bumpy right outside our school, rubbish council» What is the best answer? | The bumps are there to slow drivers down near children | The council forgot to finish the road | The bumps hide pipes that carry rainwater away from the school playground | The bumps are made by buses parking there
exam: Identify the principal process by which potholes are formed in road surfaces during the winter months. | The ingress of water into cracks, its freezing and expansion, and the subsequent removal of fragments by traffic | The contraction of asphalt by cold air until the surface disappears | The deliberate excavation of the surface by snowploughs | The sinking of heavy winter tyres into the surface
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the conduct of persons whose vehicle has broken down upon a motorway. | They shall leave the vehicle on the side away from the traffic and wait behind the barrier | They shall remain within the vehicle in the lane of traffic | They shall proceed on foot along the carriageway to the next junction | They shall stand behind the vehicle and signal to passing drivers
exam: Ascertain the reason for which salt is distributed upon roads prior to an anticipated frost. | Water containing salt freezes at a lower temperature than pure water | Salt generates heat by slow combustion upon the surface of the road during the night | Salt absorbs the entirety of the moisture present | Salt increases the adhesion of tyres to ice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the meaning of a triangular road sign in the United Kingdom? | It conveys a warning of a hazard ahead | It conveys a mandatory instruction | It conveys information concerning destinations | It is reserved for the use of cyclists
